  "result": "Fixed. The bug was a reversed comparison in `Store.save_order` (`shop/storage.py:17`).\n\n**Root cause:** `save_order` is supposed to remove any existing row for this order (so a re-save replaces it), then append the new row and write the file. But the filter was `r[\"id\"] == order.id`, which keeps *only* the row with this order's id and drops all the others. So every save erased the rest of the file, and `load()` only ever returned the last order saved.\n\n**Fix:** I changed it to `r[\"id\"] != order.id`, so all other orders are kept and only this order's old row is replaced.\n\n**Verification:**\n- I ran a manual check in a temp file: saved order 1000, saved order 1001, then moved 1000 to `paid` and saved it again. `load()` returned `[(1001, 'new'), (1000, 'paid')]`: both orders, and the re-save replaced the old row instead of adding a duplicate.\n- Test suite: pytest isn't installed here, so I ran it with `python3 -m unittest`. All 6 tests pass. None of them touch `Store`, which is why this bug got through. I can add a regression test for it in `tests/` if you'd like.",
